{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '1,110p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\\documentclass{article}\n     2\t% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n     3\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n     4\t\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n     5\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n     6\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n     7\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n     8\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n     9\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n    10\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n    11\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n    12\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n    13\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n    14\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n    15\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n    16\t}\n    17\t% --- end watermark ---\n    18\t\n    19\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n    20\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n    21\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n    22\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption}\n    23\t\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n    24\t\\title{Score-Targeted Item Selection for LLM Benchmark Prediction:\\\\Accuracy and Evaluation Limits}\n    25\t\\author{}\n    26\t\\newcommand{\\clip}{\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}}\n    27\t\\newcommand{\\sigmoid}{\\operatorname{sigmoid}}\n    28\t\\begin{document}\n    29\t\\maketitle\n    30\t\n    31\t\\begin{abstract}\n    32\tPredicting a language model's benchmark score from a few responses can reduce evaluation cost, but prediction accuracy and evaluation completeness are different requirements. We study a source-only estimator that selects items by their conditional covariance with the full score and combines ridge prediction with an optional Rasch-residual profile mixture. A frozen evaluation covers ten fixed benchmark cases, three grouped model splits, and budgets of 8, 16, and 32 items. On common available support, the estimator's mean absolute error decreases from 0.0439 to 0.0278 across these budgets; its Spearman correlation increases from 0.7787 to 0.8858. These are conditional descriptions, not qualified overall results. The evaluation completed but failed qualification: primary and reference predictions are missing for two Commonsense splits, and matched structural checks fail. The reported aggregate MAE of 0.0880 includes missing-output penalties. We separate measured prediction errors, scoring defaults, and unresolved diagnostics, establishing an evidence-bounded assessment rather than an overall selection-advantage claim.\n    33\t\\end{abstract}\n    34\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation]]\n    35\t% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/budget_mae]]\n    36\t% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/budget_spearman]]\n    37\t\n    38\t\\section{Introduction}\n    39\tFull-benchmark evaluation repeatedly measures the same item universe across language models, checkpoints, or inference settings. Historical item-level responses may therefore contain useful information about which questions distinguish model behavior. The practical question is whether a new model can answer a small, fixed subset while a source-trained estimator reconstructs its full score and preserves model rankings. This setting differs from accelerating generation: the savings come from querying fewer items, not from changing the evaluated language model.\n    40\t\n    41\tExisting benchmark-compression methods exploit response similarity, latent ability, or predictive dependencies \\citep{Vivek2024,Polo2024,Kipnis2025,Pacchiardi2024,Li2025}. A complementary design objective is to select questions directly for the scalar score of interest. An item can distinguish source response patterns without being especially informative about their average. Conversely, correlated items can remain useful if their residual variation predicts that average. This motivates conditioning on already selected items and greedily targeting the remaining covariance with the full source score.\n    42\t\n    43\tWe study this design together with a source-trained reconstruction family. A linear ridge predictor provides a simple fallback; a group-balanced mixture of Rasch-shrunk source profiles offers nonlinear response-pattern matching. Both use the same nested item prefixes, and their combination is selected using source-group holdouts. The scientific hypothesis is that source response structure can improve score reconstruction at a fixed query budget. The available evaluation does not isolate a benefit from the profile component, nor does it establish a generally advantageous selector.\n    44\t\n    45\tThe decisive evidence boundary is evaluation integrity. The frozen assessment completed but did not qualify. Missing primary predictions coincide with missing reference predictions in part of the Commonsense evidence; matched structural checks also fail there. The retained predictions can describe accuracy on available pairs, but cannot supply the missing outcomes or restore qualification. Treating penalty-scored aggregate errors as ordinary prediction errors would obscure this distinction.\n    46\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n    47\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    48\t\n    49\tOur contributions are consequently bounded: a precise description of a score-targeted, source-only reconstruction method; a common-support comparison across fixed benchmarks and item budgets; and an explicit account of how incomplete records restrict the interpretation of those comparisons. We report the unsuccessful qualification alongside the conditional results rather than selecting whichever summary gives the most favorable ordering. The resulting study addresses what these measurements establish, and what remains unmeasured.\n    50\t\n    51\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    52\t\\centering\n    53\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/method_and_evidence_overview.png}\n    54\t\\caption{Source-only item design and score reconstruction, with an explicit evidence boundary. Source responses determine a fixed nested item order, ridge fitting, and an optional residual-profile mixture. Target responses update prediction only. Measured prediction error is distinguished from missing records and failed checks; the latter cannot be repaired by conditioning on available predictions.}\n    55\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    56\t\\end{figure}\n    57\t\n    58\t\\section{Related Work}\n    59\t\\paragraph{Few-item language-model evaluation.}\n    60\tAnchor Points selects a small set of representative examples from cross-model response structure \\citep{Vivek2024}. tinyBenchmarks studies efficient score estimation with curated subsets, and metabench uses latent abilities to compress reasoning and knowledge evaluation \\citep{Polo2024,Kipnis2025}. Reference-instance assessors predict model performance on other instances \\citep{Pacchiardi2024}; learned evaluation-acquisition policies model dependencies between test outcomes \\citep{Li2025}. Our fixed source-only order is not target-adaptive acquisition. The local anchor and IRT references below are explicitly defined implementations, not certified reproductions of these published systems.\n    61\t\n    62\t\\paragraph{Design and reconstruction.}\n    63\tChoosing observations for a scalar target relates to optimal allocation in regression \\citep{Elfving1952}, whereas information criteria for a whole parameter vector address a different objective \\citep{Kiefer1960}. Our empirical covariance rule is a regularized linear-prediction heuristic, not an optimal-design theorem for shifted binary responses. Ridge regression supplies a stabilized linear estimator \\citep{Hoerl1970}; standard statistical-learning and probabilistic-modeling texts provide broader background \\citep{Hastie2009,Bishop2006}. The profile model is a finite mixture, but it does not fit mixture weights by expectation maximization \\citep{Dempster1977}. Likewise, its fixed-ability difficulty fitting should not be confused with conditional maximum-likelihood estimation \\citep{Andersen1970}.\n    64\t\n    65\t\\paragraph{Assessment under dependence and missingness.}\n    66\tCross-validation distinguishes choosing a predictor from assessing it \\citep{Stone1974,Arlot2010}; evaluating a configuration on the same validation evidence used to tune it can be optimistic \\citep{Varma2006}. Grouped validation addresses dependence, but the blocking scheme determines which extrapolation question is tested \\citep{Roberts2017}. Domain-shift theory further motivates caution about transferring source accuracy to new response distributions \\citep{BenDavid2010}. Missing-data inference requires assumptions about the missingness process \\citep{Rubin1976,Little2019}; we make no such assumptions here. HELM motivates multi-metric evaluation \\citep{Liang2023}, and distinct benchmark constructions, such as MMLU-Pro, should not be conflated with the fixed MMLU case studied here \\citep{Wang2024}. These distinctions motivate reporting error, ranking, and evidence availability separately.\n    67\t\n    68\t\\section{Method}\n    69\t\\subsection{Problem Setup and Design Rationale}\n    70\tLet $X\\in\\{0,1\\}^{S\\times N}$ contain complete item responses of source models, and let $y\\in\\{0,1\\}^{N}$ denote a target model's responses. Its reference score is $s(y)=N^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{N}y_j$. Only $y_Q$ is observable to the estimator, for a subset $Q$ of size $b\\ll N$. Source rows have supplied group labels $g(i)$; target identities, target groups, and full target scores are unavailable during fitting and prediction. The goal is a bounded estimate $\\hat s_b(y_Q)$ with small absolute and squared score error and accurate target-model ordering.\n    71\t\n    72\tThe studied budgets are $b\\in\\{8,16,32\\}$. A single source-only order gives nested prefixes $Q_b$, with no repeated items. This is not a claim that these budgets suffice for every benchmark. The rationale is to combine task-directed acquisition with conservative reconstruction: selection targets the full-score functional, while a tuned linear fallback limits reliance on an overly specific source-profile model. Equal total source-group mass reduces the influence of densely represented groups in selection and profile priors, without asserting that groups perfectly recover training ancestry.\n    73\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/budgets]]\n    74\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L1-L28]]\n    75\t\n    76\t\\subsection{Score-Targeted Fixed Prefixes}\n    77\tFor $G$ source groups with sizes $n_g$, define $w_i=(G n_{g(i)})^{-1}$, source item means $\\mu_j=\\sum_i w_iX_{ij}$, and full source scores $t_i=N^{-1}\\sum_jX_{ij}$. With $\\bar t=\\sum_iw_it_i$, form\n    78\t\\begin{equation}\n    79\t Z_{ij}=\\sqrt{w_i}(X_{ij}-\\mu_j),\\qquad\n    80\t u_i=\\sqrt{w_i}(t_i-\\bar t),\\quad\n    81\t C=Z^\\top Z+D,\\quad c=Z^\\top u,\n    82\t\\end{equation}\n    83\twhere $D_{jj}=0.1\\max\\{\\mu_j(1-\\mu_j),0.05\\}$. For the selected prefix $Q$, residual covariance and variance are\n    84\t\\begin{equation}\n    85\t c_{j\\mid Q}=c_j-C_{jQ}C_{QQ}^{-1}c_Q,\\qquad\n    86\t v_{j\\mid Q}=C_{jj}-C_{jQ}C_{QQ}^{-1}C_{Qj}.\n    87\t\\end{equation}\n    88\tThe next item maximizes $c_{j\\mid Q}^{\\,2}/\\max(v_{j\\mid Q},10^{-12})$ among unselected items. This is the reduction in residual variance for a linear scalar-score predictor under the empirical covariance model. It is neither an information guarantee for the true target distribution nor a causal statement about particular questions.\n    89\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L57-L91]]\n    90\t\n    91\tThe implementation updates residual quantities through a low-rank pivoted factorization instead of materializing the dense item covariance. Selection is rebuilt using only training groups inside every source-validation fold. The final fit repeats it on all supplied source rows. Crucially, observing target answers never changes the item order or its hyperparameters.\n    92\t\n    93\t\\subsection{Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction}\n    94\tThe linear branch regresses source full scores on the selected binary responses. Centering features at their unweighted source mean $\\bar x_Q$ and scores at $\\bar t_{\\rm row}$ gives\n    95\t\\begin{equation}\n    96\t \\beta_b=(X_{Q,c}^{\\top}X_{Q,c}+\\lambda I)^{-1}X_{Q,c}^{\\top}(t-\\bar t_{\\rm row}),\\qquad\n    97\t \\hat s^{\\rm lin}_b=\\clip\\!\\left[\\bar t_{\\rm row}+\\gamma(y_Q-\\bar x_Q)^\\top\\beta_b\\right].\n    98\t\\end{equation}\n    99\tThe ridge fit itself is row-unweighted; group balancing is used for selecting configurations, not silently substituted into this regression. Per-budget $\\lambda\\in\\{0.1,1,10\\}$ and $\\gamma\\in\\{0,0.5,1\\}$ minimize source-held-out group-weighted squared error.\n   100\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L94-L100]]\n   101\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L200-L270]]\n   102\t\n   103\t% @evidence[[reference_consultation.md#targeted-irt-foundations]]\n   104\tThe nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch response model, a logistic ability--difficulty family \\citep[Chap.~2]{Baker2001}. Our custom fit fixes source abilities at $\\theta_i=\\operatorname{logit}[(\\sum_jX_{ij}+0.5)/(N+1)]$. Item difficulties $d_j$ are fitted by group-weighted Bernoulli likelihood with a quadratic penalty. Write $r_{ij}=\\sigmoid(\\theta_i-d_j)$ and\n   105\t\\begin{equation}\n   106\t p_{ij}=\\operatorname{clip}_{[\\epsilon,1-\\epsilon]}\n   107\t       [(1-\\rho)r_{ij}+\\rho X_{ij}],\\qquad\n   108\t p_{i\\ell j}=\\sigmoid[\\operatorname{logit}(p_{ij})+a_\\ell].\n   109\t\\end{equation}\n   110\tThe offsets are $a_\\ell=h(-2,-1,0,1,2)_\\ell$ with prior proportions $(1,4,6,4,1)/16$. Each source group's total prior mass is equal; within a group it is divided equally across source rows. Thus this is a mixture of source-derived response profiles and ability offsets, not a new language-model architecture.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
