{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"ls -ld task results 2>/dev/null; cat .agentic_paper_workspace.json; jq '.references[4] | {citation_key,title,abstract,source,provenance}' references_provenance.json; jq '.references[25]' references_provenance.json; jq '{normalization,limitations,weights,claim_policy}' source_evidence/final_data_manifest.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"global_memory_dir\": \"work/results/t07/codex/20261113_154315/investigation\",\n  \"output_dir\": \"work/results/t07/codex/20261113_154315/paper\",\n  \"results_dir\": \"work/results/t07/codex/20261113_154315/investigation\",\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"task_dir\": \"work/results/t07/codex/20261113_154315/task\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Li2025\",\n  \"title\": \"Active Evaluation Acquisition for Efficient LLM Benchmarking\",\n  \"abstract\": \"As large language models (LLMs) become increasingly versatile, numerous large scale benchmarks have been developed to thoroughly assess their capabilities. These benchmarks typically consist of diverse datasets and prompts to evaluate different aspects of LLM performance. However, comprehensive evaluations on hundreds or thousands of prompts incur tremendous costs in terms of computation, money, and time. In this work, we investigate strategies to improve evaluation efficiency by selecting a subset of examples from each benchmark using a learned policy. Our approach models the dependencies across test examples, allowing accurate prediction of the evaluation outcomes for the remaining examples based on the outcomes of the selected ones. Consequently, we only need to acquire the actual evaluation outcomes for the selected subset. We rigorously explore various subset selection policies and introduce a novel RL-based policy that leverages the captured dependencies. Empirical results demonstrate that our approach significantly reduces the number of evaluation prompts required while maintaining accurate performance estimates compared to previous methods.\",\n  \"source\": null,\n  \"provenance\": null\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"ref.eric.ED458219\",\n  \"paper_id\": \"ED458219\",\n  \"citation_key\": \"Baker2001\",\n  \"title\": \"The Basics of Item Response Theory\",\n  \"year\": 2001,\n  \"venue\": \"ERIC Clearinghouse on Assessment and Evaluation\",\n  \"query\": \"Exact authoritative source: ERIC ED458219, Rasch/2PL and item-information foundations\",\n  \"source_url\": \"https://eric.ed.gov/?id=ED458219\",\n  \"fulltext_url\": \"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED458219.pdf\",\n  \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-13T19:06:26Z\",\n  \"fulltext_sha256\": \"d310bb6600fc70597b7d7c96280923408ad108e6517d53fdb3bb66fc0d929627\",\n  \"authors\": [\n    \"Frank B. Baker\"\n  ],\n  \"edition\": \"Second\",\n  \"isbn\": \"1-886047-03-0\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"ERIC metadata plus visually inspected scanned PDF: copyright/title page (PDF 4), contents (PDF 7-8), response-model introduction (PDF 30), 2PL equation 2-1 (printed 22/PDF 31), Rasch equation 2-2 (printed 25/PDF 34), and 2PL item-information equation 6-3 (printed 111/PDF 120). No citation links traversed.\",\n  \"use_limits\": \"Model-family definitions only, not certification of custom estimation, official baseline reproduction, uncertainty calibration or component benefit.\"\n}\n{\n  \"normalization\": \"NFKC; canonical line endings; Unicode whitespace collapse; SHA256 UTF8; stem-only conservative duplicate exclusion plus ordered and sorted-choice signatures; HELM reference/explanation suffix stripped at newline A. for gsm/math/legalbench.\",\n  \"limitations\": [\n    \"Family grouping is a conservative heuristic; metadata cannot prove all model training lineages.\",\n    \"Column alignment relies on supplied SOURCES reconstruction attestations and pinned raw checksums, not newly downloaded model outputs.\",\n    \"Raw text is not redistributed; adapters are fixed at staging. Hashes and mappings retained; task-author-supplied metadata needed to independently repeat raw audit.\",\n    \"No novel-method superiority gate; valid negative results remain eligible.\",\n    \"Bootstrap treats benchmark item universes as fixed; no population-of-items claim or independent-benchmark significance claim is supported.\"\n  ],\n  \"weights\": \"Equal checkpoints; equal statistical seeds within case; equal cases within each observed family; equal observed families. Replay identity. Control types each 1/4; missing rate denominator complete episode count.\",\n  \"claim_policy\": \"Validity and non-vacuous structural controls are required; no accuracy improvement or adaptivity gate. Single primary confirmatory contrast is aggregate candidate-minus-anchor error on fixed item universes. 95 percent paired model-lineage/seed cluster interval. All individual budget, other baseline and subgroup contrasts are exploratory unless Holm familywise alpha .05 is applied to the full frozen 12 method-budget comparisons after freeze. No benchmark/item-population independence claim.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
